1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:06,121
What does it take to engineer an AI
customer support platform that can serve

2
00:00:06,171 --> 00:00:09,368
multiple businesses while keeping their
knowledge, conversations,

3
00:00:09,418 --> 00:00:11,532
and customer data isolated?

4
00:00:11,582 --> 00:00:16,221
This case study shows the working product,
the architecture behind it,

5
00:00:16,271 --> 00:00:20,550
what broke along the way, and what is
still unproven.

6
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:24,989
Customers ask the same questions again and
again.

7
00:00:25,039 --> 00:00:29,719
The answers usually exist, spread across
policy documents and web pages,

8
00:00:29,769 --> 00:00:33,358
but someone has to find them each time.

9
00:00:33,408 --> 00:00:38,089
A support assistant grounded in a
business's own material can help.

10
00:00:38,139 --> 00:00:42,092
And if one shared platform serves many
separate businesses,

11
00:00:42,142 --> 00:00:47,550
every answer has to come from the right
one, and only that one.

12
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:52,950
Here is the real application, running
locally with fictional data.

13
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:57,450
An owner signs in and lands on the
organization dashboard.

14
00:00:57,700 --> 00:01:01,450
A short wizard creates a chatbot.

15
00:01:01,700 --> 00:01:06,060
Documents are uploaded, and a background
worker takes each one from queued,

16
00:01:06,110 --> 00:01:07,950
to processing, to ready.

17
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:13,950
You can inspect the passages it produced
and test which ones a question retrieves.

18
00:01:14,200 --> 00:01:18,950
Websites can be added too; here the
crawler refuses a private network address.

19
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:23,801
In the playground, a question streams back
with a numbered source.

20
00:01:23,851 --> 00:01:25,513
One important note:

21
00:01:25,563 --> 00:01:27,909
this is a deterministic demonstration.

22
00:01:27,959 --> 00:01:32,018
The reply is a fixed sentence, not an AI
answer.

23
00:01:32,068 --> 00:01:36,469
What is real is the retrieval, the
streaming and the citation.

24
00:01:36,519 --> 00:01:39,550
An unrelated question gets a fallback
instead.

25
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:44,900
Once the bot is active and the website's
origin is verified,

26
00:01:44,950 --> 00:01:48,050
a visitor asks through the embedded
widget.

27
00:01:48,100 --> 00:01:52,600
The team sees that conversation in the
inbox, marks it resolved,

28
00:01:52,650 --> 00:01:55,400
and analytics update from stored events.

29
00:01:55,450 --> 00:01:58,550
The figures here are synthetic
demonstration data.

30
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:02,046
The system is a modular monolith:

31
00:02:02,096 --> 00:02:06,091
one Python codebase and one database, run
as several processes.

32
00:02:06,141 --> 00:02:08,787
Think of it in four layers.

33
00:02:08,837 --> 00:02:10,135
First, experience:

34
00:02:10,185 --> 00:02:12,494
a Next.js and React dashboard,

35
00:02:12,544 --> 00:02:17,550
and a small widget that opens an isolated
frame on a customer's site.

36
00:02:18,400 --> 00:02:20,044
Second, the application.

37
00:02:20,094 --> 00:02:22,078
FastAPI handles ordinary requests.

38
00:02:22,128 --> 00:02:26,144
Dashboard requests are authenticated and
checked against the member's role,

39
00:02:26,194 --> 00:02:30,550
and chat answers come back as a stream of
server-sent events.

40
00:02:31,400 --> 00:02:33,500
Third, data and processing.

41
00:02:33,550 --> 00:02:37,083
PostgreSQL holds relational data and
vectors with pgvector.

42
00:02:37,133 --> 00:02:40,308
Private object storage keeps the original
files.

43
00:02:40,358 --> 00:02:43,533
Redis carries the queue to Celery workers,

44
00:02:43,583 --> 00:02:48,550
and a scheduler triggers a recovery sweep
on its own maintenance worker.

45
00:02:49,400 --> 00:02:50,708
Fourth, intelligence:

46
00:02:50,758 --> 00:02:53,423
document processing, retrieval and prompt
construction,

47
00:02:53,473 --> 00:02:56,477
with adapters for language and embedding
models.

48
00:02:56,527 --> 00:03:00,550
This is code inside those same processes,
not separate services.

49
00:03:01,400 --> 00:03:02,400
Together:

50
00:03:02,450 --> 00:03:06,600
synchronous requests, streamed answers and
background jobs, in Docker containers.

51
00:03:06,650 --> 00:03:09,750
Several containers do not make this
microservices.

52
00:03:09,800 --> 00:03:12,550
The detailed diagram shows each
connection.

53
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:16,550
Retrieval augmented generation means
finding evidence first,

54
00:03:16,600 --> 00:03:18,683
then writing from it.

55
00:03:18,733 --> 00:03:22,950
A file or web page is extracted into text,
cleaned,

56
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:27,217
and split into overlapping chunks that
never cross a heading.

57
00:03:27,267 --> 00:03:32,550
Each chunk gets an embedding, a list of
numbers, stored beside the text.

58
00:03:33,400 --> 00:03:36,156
A question is searched two ways:

59
00:03:36,206 --> 00:03:39,663
vector search compares embeddings, keyword
search matches words,

60
00:03:39,713 --> 00:03:42,469
and the two rankings are merged.

61
00:03:42,519 --> 00:03:46,678
Retrieval is always scoped to one
organization and one chatbot.

62
00:03:46,728 --> 00:03:49,834
The prompt is assembled, the model
streams,

63
00:03:49,884 --> 00:03:53,341
and citations are validated on the way
out.

64
00:03:53,391 --> 00:03:57,550
Only sources that actually entered the
prompt can be cited.

65
00:03:58,400 --> 00:04:02,603
Citation provenance proves a source was
shown to the model,

66
00:04:02,653 --> 00:04:05,438
not that it supports the sentence.

67
00:04:05,488 --> 00:04:08,627
Offline, with a word-matching stand-in for
embeddings,

68
00:04:08,677 --> 00:04:13,589
the answer appeared in the top five for
eighty-five percent of cases,

69
00:04:13,639 --> 00:04:16,069
below the ninety percent target.

70
00:04:16,119 --> 00:04:18,550
Real model accuracy is untested.

71
00:04:19,400 --> 00:04:21,501
Take two fictional businesses.

72
00:04:21,551 --> 00:04:25,085
Each has its own chatbot, documents and
conversations.

73
00:04:25,135 --> 00:04:29,028
Here the second owner opens the first
one's address,

74
00:04:29,078 --> 00:04:31,537
and gets organization not found.

75
00:04:32,400 --> 00:04:34,440
Several layers enforce this.

76
00:04:34,490 --> 00:04:38,967
Sessions live on the server, in a cookie
scripts cannot read.

77
00:04:39,017 --> 00:04:42,102
Roles decide what each member may do.

78
00:04:42,152 --> 00:04:44,888
Queries are scoped to the organization,

79
00:04:44,938 --> 00:04:48,022
and PostgreSQL enforces forced row-level
security underneath,

80
00:04:48,072 --> 00:04:52,550
so a missed filter is not enough to leak a
row.

81
00:04:53,400 --> 00:04:57,211
Public widgets receive short-lived tokens
limited to one conversation,

82
00:04:57,261 --> 00:05:00,721
and visitors get only the fields they
need.

83
00:05:00,771 --> 00:05:04,232
Forwarding headers are trusted only from
listed proxies,

84
00:05:04,282 --> 00:05:07,040
and AI providers must use HTTPS.

85
00:05:07,090 --> 00:05:10,550
This is tested design, not a security
certification.

86
00:05:11,400 --> 00:05:16,687
An audit then found problems that the
existing, passing tests had missed.

87
00:05:16,737 --> 00:05:23,550
Here are eleven of the hardest, some from
the audit and some found while fixing it.

88
00:05:24,400 --> 00:05:28,275
First, ten simultaneous chats exhausted
the database connection pool:

89
00:05:28,325 --> 00:05:32,201
each request held one connection while
asking for another.

90
00:05:32,251 --> 00:05:36,483
Releasing it early, short transactions and
bounded admission fixed it.

91
00:05:36,533 --> 00:05:41,123
At one hundred concurrent requests, sixty-
seven were served, thirty-three refused,

92
00:05:41,173 --> 00:05:42,550
none failed.

93
00:05:43,400 --> 00:05:48,082
Second, a source cut from the prompt could
still be cited,

94
00:05:48,132 --> 00:05:53,178
because citations were checked against
what was selected, not what was shown.

95
00:05:53,228 --> 00:05:57,182
Now only rendered sources can be cited,
even mid-stream.

96
00:05:57,232 --> 00:06:01,550
Whether a source truly supports a sentence
is still unverified.

97
00:06:02,400 --> 00:06:06,428
Third, production settings accepted a
plain HTTP embedding endpoint,

98
00:06:06,478 --> 00:06:09,765
which could send keys and documents
unencrypted.

99
00:06:09,815 --> 00:06:14,213
Providers now require HTTPS, refuse
redirects and ignore environment proxies.

100
00:06:14,263 --> 00:06:17,550
No real provider endpoint has been
exercised.

101
00:06:18,400 --> 00:06:21,885
Fourth, the server trusted forwarding
headers from anyone,

102
00:06:21,935 --> 00:06:26,834
so a client could rewrite its own address
and dodge rate limits.

103
00:06:26,884 --> 00:06:30,722
Now no proxy is trusted unless it is
listed.

104
00:06:30,772 --> 00:06:33,550
The real production ingress remains
unverified.

105
00:06:34,400 --> 00:06:38,376
Fifth, a hostile document could tie up a
worker.

106
00:06:38,426 --> 00:06:44,231
Each parse now runs in a fresh process
with time, CPU and memory limits.

107
00:06:44,281 --> 00:06:47,890
That is containment, not yet a security
sandbox:

108
00:06:47,940 --> 00:06:51,550
the child shares the worker's user and
network.

109
00:06:52,400 --> 00:06:57,107
Sixth, a superseded job could resume and
publish over newer work.

110
00:06:57,157 --> 00:07:02,231
Every claim now carries an attempt number,
and stale attempts change nothing.

111
00:07:02,281 --> 00:07:05,159
Killed workers were recovered in tests,

112
00:07:05,209 --> 00:07:09,550
although with default settings recovery
can take about seventeen minutes.

113
00:07:10,400 --> 00:07:13,107
Seventh, found while fixing that one,

114
00:07:13,157 --> 00:07:16,554
a stale crawl could overwrite newer stored
pages.

115
00:07:16,604 --> 00:07:20,001
Every write now gets a new object key,

116
00:07:20,051 --> 00:07:24,137
published only by the attempt that still
owns the job,

117
00:07:24,187 --> 00:07:26,550
and unpublished uploads are reclaimed.

118
00:07:27,400 --> 00:07:30,715
Eighth, the inbox silently dropped older
messages,

119
00:07:30,765 --> 00:07:36,324
restarted at page one on a bad cursor, and
could show stale data.

120
00:07:36,374 --> 00:07:41,185
Cursors are now scoped and checked, older
messages load on request,

121
00:07:41,235 --> 00:07:44,550
and screens reset when the organization
changes.

122
00:07:45,400 --> 00:07:49,931
Ninth, installing the widget twice broke
it, nothing could remove it,

123
00:07:49,981 --> 00:07:53,102
and visitors could see internal usage
fields.

124
00:07:53,152 --> 00:07:56,979
A registry now keeps one instance, destroy
cleans up,

125
00:07:57,029 --> 00:08:00,855
and public responses carry only what the
widget needs.

126
00:08:00,905 --> 00:08:05,436
The loader is five thousand two hundred
and forty-eight bytes compressed.

127
00:08:05,486 --> 00:08:07,550
Tested in Chrome only.

128
00:08:08,400 --> 00:08:11,715
Tenth, recovery shared the busy ingestion
workers,

129
00:08:11,765 --> 00:08:15,828
and the scheduler's health check did not
check scheduling.

130
00:08:15,878 --> 00:08:22,185
Recovery now has its own queue and worker,
and health means tasks are being accepted.

131
00:08:22,235 --> 00:08:25,550
One earlier scheduler crash is still
unexplained.

132
00:08:26,400 --> 00:08:29,388
Eleventh, a backup only counts once
restored.

133
00:08:29,438 --> 00:08:34,113
The rehearsal compared counts, hashes and
permissions, and exposed a real defect:

134
00:08:34,162 --> 00:08:36,813
the restored database regained delete
rights.

135
00:08:36,863 --> 00:08:38,500
That is fixed.

136
00:08:38,550 --> 00:08:42,550
Off-site copies, alerting and production-
sized data are not covered.

137
00:08:43,400 --> 00:08:45,883
The method was audit driven.

138
00:08:45,933 --> 00:08:49,864
Build the initial system, measure a
baseline, record findings,

139
00:08:49,914 --> 00:08:54,931
fix one at a time with a failing test
first where possible,

140
00:08:54,981 --> 00:08:58,550
stabilize performance, then validate the
whole thing together.

141
00:08:59,400 --> 00:09:01,859
The audit raised seven findings.

142
00:09:01,909 --> 00:09:04,368
All seven now pass locally.

143
00:09:04,418 --> 00:09:06,161
The reported results:

144
00:09:06,211 --> 00:09:10,821
twelve hundred and nine backend tests,
seventeen proxy tests run separately,

145
00:09:10,871 --> 00:09:15,481
two hundred and sixty-two frontend unit
tests, and seventeen browser journeys:

146
00:09:15,531 --> 00:09:17,631
fifteen hundred and five.

147
00:09:19,400 --> 00:09:21,763
The path was not clean.

148
00:09:21,813 --> 00:09:24,865
Earlier checkpoints kept failing tests on
record,

149
00:09:24,915 --> 00:09:28,656
and a latency gate was missed before it
held.

150
00:09:28,706 --> 00:09:33,137
To check the tests themselves, fixes were
deliberately broken ten times,

151
00:09:33,187 --> 00:09:35,550
and each break was caught.

152
00:09:36,400 --> 00:09:39,850
At one hundred concurrent chats, sixty-
seven succeeded,

153
00:09:39,900 --> 00:09:43,700
thirty-three received a controlled
rejection and none failed unexpectedly.

154
00:09:43,750 --> 00:09:48,250
Successful responses had a ninety-fifth
percentile of about one point seven

155
00:09:48,300 --> 00:09:49,300
seconds.

156
00:09:49,350 --> 00:09:52,800
The gate passed ten of ten declared runs,

157
00:09:52,850 --> 00:09:55,950
once with only twenty-five milliseconds to
spare.

158
00:09:56,000 --> 00:10:01,550
This is an offline provider on one laptop,
not real model or server performance.

159
00:10:02,400 --> 00:10:08,091
The self-audit score moved from eighty and
a half to eighty-five point seven five.

160
00:10:08,141 --> 00:10:11,321
These are local results, not independent
certification,

161
00:10:11,371 --> 00:10:14,550
and hosted CI has not run yet.

162
00:10:15,400 --> 00:10:18,223
This is my undergraduate portfolio
project,

163
00:10:18,273 --> 00:10:22,534
built with AI coding assistants including
Codex and Claude Code,

164
00:10:22,584 --> 00:10:27,203
so I will not claim every line was typed
by hand.

165
00:10:27,253 --> 00:10:31,154
What I want you to judge is the process:

166
00:10:31,204 --> 00:10:34,028
written requirements with explicit
acceptance rules,

167
00:10:34,078 --> 00:10:38,697
a baseline before changes, an audit,
focused fixes with retained failures,

168
00:10:38,747 --> 00:10:42,289
and decisions held for approval instead of
assumed.

169
00:10:42,339 --> 00:10:45,881
Ask me about any trade-off in this video,

170
00:10:45,931 --> 00:10:50,550
including the ones that did not work out
the first time.

171
00:10:51,400 --> 00:10:53,094
Where it stands:

172
00:10:53,144 --> 00:10:57,976
the core engineering is validated locally
and all seven audit findings pass.

173
00:10:58,026 --> 00:11:01,812
One migration policy decision still blocks
formal milestone closure.

174
00:11:01,862 --> 00:11:04,601
Real AI evaluation, semantic answer
quality,

175
00:11:04,651 --> 00:11:08,088
hosted CI and production deployment all
remain open.

176
00:11:08,138 --> 00:11:12,273
Offline retrieval reaches eighty-five
percent against a ninety percent target,

177
00:11:12,323 --> 00:11:15,412
and the parser is contained, not
sandboxed.

178
00:11:15,462 --> 00:11:18,550
Those are the roadmap, not existing
features.

179
00:11:19,400 --> 00:11:23,060
A multi-tenant AI customer support
platform with retrieval augmented

180
00:11:23,110 --> 00:11:24,072
generation.

181
00:11:24,122 --> 00:11:27,107
Software engineering, full-stack
development and AI engineering.

182
00:11:27,157 --> 00:11:31,154
The full case study, with its evidence and
its limitations,

183
00:11:31,204 --> 00:11:34,526
is on my portfolio at venuja dot online.

184
00:11:34,576 --> 00:11:36,550
Thank you for watching.
